\emph{Step 2 (the massive propagator to order $m^{2}$).} On the flat field the Hadamard form (Fact \claimref{synthesis.imported-hadamard-parametrix-of-the-squared-dirac-operator-leads-with-van-vleck-root-times-spin-transport}) gives the scalar kernel $\Delta =  rac{1}{4\pi^{2}s} + V\ln s + W$ with $V$, $W$ smooth and $V(0) = Am^{2}$; the Klein-Gordon equation $(-\partial^{2} + m^{2})\Delta = 0$ off the origin at order $m^{2}$ reads $-A\,\partial^{2}\ln s +  rac{1}{4\pi^{2}s} = 0$, and $\partial^{2}\ln s = 4/s$, so $A = 1/(16\pi^{2})$. With $S = (-\partial\!\!\!/ + m)\Delta$ (so that $(\partial\!\!\!/ + m)S = \delta$), $S = \alpha x +  eta + R$ with $\alpha =  rac{1}{2\pi^{2}s^{2}} -  rac{m^{2}}{8\pi^{2}s}$, $ eta =  rac{m}{4\pi^{2}s}$, and $R$ the rest: its vector part is $x$ times $O(m^{4}\ln s)$ and its scalar part $O(m^{3}\ln s)$, both of scaling degree at most $0$ (a power of $\ln s$ does not raise the scaling degree).

\emph{Step 3 (the delta normalisation).} On the Euclidean section $1/s$ is locally integrable and harmonic off the origin. By the divergence theorem on a ball of radius $r$, $\int\partial^{2}(1/s)\,\varphi 	o \varphi(0)\cdot rac{d}{dr}(r^{-2})\cdot|S^{3}|r^{3} = \varphi(0)(-2r^{-3})(2\pi^{2}r^{3}) = -4\pi^{2}\varphi(0)$, so $\partial^{2}(1/s) = -4\pi^{2}\delta$.

