u}\partial^{2}) = 0$ identically, so the two displayed parts are transverse as distributions. The remainder has scaling degree at most $2 < 4$, so its extension is unique by the imported theorem \cite{BrunettiFredenhagen2000} (Fact \claimref{synthesis.imported-epstein-glaser-extension-theorem-and-ward-preserving-time-ordered-products}(ii)); its divergence is the unique extension of the zero kernel (scaling degree at most $3 < 4$), hence zero. So $ ar\Pi$ is covariant and transverse: the Ward identity holds. The parameter $\mu$ enters only through $L_{\mu}$, and $\mu\,\partial_{\mu}(L_{\mu}/s) = 2/s$, so by Step 3
