\emph{Step 6 (the ambiguity spaces).} By the imported theorem two extensions of a kernel with degree of divergence $\omega$ differ by $\sum_{|a| \le \omega}c_{a}\partial^{a}\delta$; in momentum space, by a polynomial of degree at most $\omega$ in the momenta, with the kernel's tensor or spinor type. A covariant extension procedure leaves only covariant differences. The check enumerates, for each kernel, the polynomial maps of the right degree and type as linear combinations of monomials in the components of the momenta and polarisations times basis blades of $\Cl(1,3)$, imposes infinitesimal covariance under the six bivector generators $B$ (vectors $v \mapsto  rac12(Bv - vB)$, values $X \mapsto  rac12(BX - XB)$), and computes the dimension of the solution space as the number of unknowns minus an exact rank. The results are those stated in (iii): $3$ for the photon two-point ($g$, $Q(q)g$, $qq$), of which the transverse condition $\Pi(q)(q, b) = 0$ leaves $1$, the combination $Q(q)g - qq$, which is $Z_{3}$; $4$ for the self-energy ($1$, $I_{4}$, $q$, $qI_{4}$, left multiplications), of which the reflection $v \mapsto \gamma_{0}v\gamma_{0}$, $X \mapsto \gamma_{0}X\gamma_{0}$ keeps $1$ and $q$ ($\gamma_{0}I_{4}\gamma_{0} = -I_{4}$), the mass shift $\delta m$ and $Z_{2}$; $2$ for the vertex ($a$, $aI_{4}$), $1$ under the reflection. The Ward-Takahashi identity $q_{\mu}\Lambda^{\mu}(p + q, p) = \Sigma(p) - \Sigma(p + q)$ applied to the ambiguities $\Lambda = c_{V}a$ and $\Sigma = c_{m} + c_{2}q$ reads $c_{V}q = -c_{2}q$: one condition, leaving the two-dimensional space $(c_{m}, c_{2})$ with $c_{V} = -c_{2}$, which is $Z_{1} = Z_{2}$ in the convention $\Lambda_{\mathrm{ct}} = (Z_{1} - 1)\gamma$, $\Sigma_{\mathrm{ct}} 
i -(Z_{2} - 1)q$ (computed jointly: dimension $2$ under the reflection, $4$ without, the two pseudoscalar terms being tied in the same way). For three photons the covariant trilinear forms of degree at most $1$ in $(q_{1}, q_{2})$, $q_{3} = -q_{1} - q_{2}$, span $8$ dimensions ($(a_{i}\cdot a_{j})(a_{k}\cdot q)$ and $\epsilon(a_{1}, a_{2}, a_{3}, q)$, $q \in \{q_{1}, q_{2}\}$), and the transverse conditions on any two legs ($a_{i} = q_{i}$) leave none; for four photons the constant covariant forms span $4$ dimensions (three products of $g$ and one $\epsilon$), and transversality on one leg leaves none. Hence, if covariant extensions satisfying the Ward identities and the reflection exist, any two differ exactly by $Z_{3}$, $\delta m$ and $Z_{2} = Z_{1}$, the counterterms of electrodynamics, and Furry's theorem is not needed to fix the three-photon kernel. Existence is proved here only for the vacuum polarisation (Step 5); for the self-energy and vertex, the four-photon and the three-current kernels it is the known result of the causal approach (Scharf \cite{Scharf1995}; Fact \claimref{synthesis.imported-epstein-glaser-extension-theorem-and-ward-preserving-time-ordered-products}(iii)), not proved here. The self-energy and vertex kernels contain the photon propagator in a covariant gauge, so these parts are read under the Krein-form premise \claimref{synthesis.krein-form-premise-indefinite-inner-product-for-gauge-fields}; the counting itself uses only covariance. The same eight-dimensional space contains every covariant ambiguity of a three-current kernel with one axial and two vector legs (the $\epsilon$ terms included), so at most one covariant extension conserves the two vector currents, and every such extension (one exists by the imported result) has the same axial divergence; the value computed with Pauli-Villars fields in the triangle result \claimref{synthesis.triangle-anomaly-in-the-spacetime-algebra-is-the-pseudoscalar-part-of-the-field-square-and-cancels-per